WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.063 position:50% size:92% align:center
Five inputs need five array updates. The

00:00:03.063 --> 00:00:05.649 position:50% size:92% align:center
supplied launch creates two blocks of four

00:00:05.649 --> 00:00:07.619 position:50% size:92% align:center
threads, so eight threads start.

00:00:07.839 --> 00:00:10.345 position:50% size:92% align:center
Follow where those threads fit in the GPU,

00:00:10.345 --> 00:00:13.151 position:50% size:92% align:center
including the three that have no input element.

00:00:15.171 --> 00:00:18.125 position:50% size:92% align:center
An SM is a streaming multiprocessor: hardware

00:00:18.125 --> 00:00:21.080 position:50% size:92% align:center
that executes the threads of assigned blocks.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.855 position:50% size:92% align:center
Both blocks are drawn on one SM here as a

00:00:23.855 --> 00:00:26.721 position:50% size:92% align:center
possible placement. The launch does not assign

00:00:26.721 --> 00:00:29.151 position:50% size:92% align:center
each thread its own physical processor.

00:00:31.171 --> 00:00:33.839 position:50% size:92% align:center
A warp is a group of up to thirty-two threads

00:00:33.839 --> 00:00:35.796 position:50% size:92% align:center
from one block whose instructions

00:00:35.796 --> 00:00:36.982 position:50% size:92% align:center
are issued together.

00:00:37.202 --> 00:00:40.171 position:50% size:92% align:center
A four-thread block occupies a partially filled

00:00:40.171 --> 00:00:43.140 position:50% size:92% align:center
warp. Threads from the second block cannot fill

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.351 position:50% size:92% align:center
the first block’s unused positions.

00:00:47.371 --> 00:00:50.144 position:50% size:92% align:center
All eight launched threads calculate their

00:00:50.144 --> 00:00:53.048 position:50% size:92% align:center
indices. Only indices below five execute the

00:00:53.048 --> 00:00:55.094 position:50% size:92% align:center
guarded array reads and writes.

00:00:55.314 --> 00:00:57.473 position:50% size:92% align:center
Threads five, six and seven skip those

00:00:57.473 --> 00:00:59.746 position:50% size:92% align:center
operations. They are different from warp

00:00:59.746 --> 00:01:01.735 position:50% size:92% align:center
positions that were never launched.

00:01:03.755 --> 00:01:06.360 position:50% size:92% align:center
Consider changing threadsPerBlock from four to

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.342 position:50% size:92% align:center
eight. There are still five inputs.

00:01:08.562 --> 00:01:10.995 position:50% size:92% align:center
Pause and predict the number of blocks and

00:01:10.995 --> 00:01:12.965 position:50% size:92% align:center
partially filled warps. Should the

00:01:12.965 --> 00:01:14.471 position:50% size:92% align:center
five output values change?

00:01:20.291 --> 00:01:23.074 position:50% size:92% align:center
The launch now needs one block of eight threads,

00:01:23.074 --> 00:01:25.219 position:50% size:92% align:center
so it uses one partially filled warp.

00:01:25.439 --> 00:01:28.355 position:50% size:92% align:center
Five threads still perform the array operations

00:01:28.355 --> 00:01:31.208 position:50% size:92% align:center
and three skip them. The grouping changed, not

00:01:31.208 --> 00:01:32.821 position:50% size:92% align:center
the requested calculation.

00:01:34.841 --> 00:01:37.924 position:50% size:92% align:center
In the CUDA source editor, set threadsPerBlock

00:01:37.924 --> 00:01:40.269 position:50% size:92% align:center
to eight and click Compile and run.

00:01:40.489 --> 00:01:43.481 position:50% size:92% align:center
Trace CUDA calls shows one block of eight

00:01:43.481 --> 00:01:46.838 position:50% size:92% align:center
threads. Console’s five outputs stay the same;

00:01:46.838 --> 00:01:49.683 position:50% size:92% align:center
the warp diagram explains grouping, not

00:01:49.683 --> 00:01:51.435 position:50% size:92% align:center
measured execution time.
